WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.180
We all want to eat
the kinds of foods

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.100
that make us feel
better and live longer.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.220
But there’s so much conflicting
information out there,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.680
so many nutrition opinions.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.920
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.720
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.360
It’s my job to give you
the information you need

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.760
to make the healthiest
choices possible.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.100
Did you know that as we
age our telomeres shorten?

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.280
Telomeres are a series
of DNA sequences

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.360
that protect the ends of our chromosomes
from becoming frayed or tangled.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.200
As it turns out, a plant
based diet and lifestyle

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.420
can effectively reverse cellular
aging by elongating telomeres.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.740
Here’s the story.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.820
Identifying simple strategies
to prevent or delay age-related

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.460
diseases is a major
public health concern.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.000
But how could you measure
the effects of such strategies?

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.740
Well, telomere length is a reliable
hallmark of biological aging

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.656
and the risk of developing
age-related chronic diseases.

00:01:01.708 --> 00:01:03.120
What is a telomere,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.640
and why does it matter
how long they are?

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:11.400
Telomere comes from the Greek for
"end" "part" of our chromosomes.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.220
Telomeres cap the ends
of our chromosomes

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.460
like shoelace tips to essentially
keep our DNA from fraying.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.480
Telomere length is important since
there's a minimum length required,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.500
but every time our cells divide,
a bit of the telomere is lost

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.860
and once they get too short,
the cell can die.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.060
That's why telomeres are sometimes
called the molecular clock of cells.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.200
Every year they get shorter and
shorter, kind of like life's fuse,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.920
but in some people that fuse
burns faster than in others.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.120
Accelerated telomere shortening
has been identified as a key biomarker

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.900
for accelerated aging, disease risk,
and diminished longevity.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.280
But there's some good news.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.940
Telomere shortening can be
counteracted by an enzyme

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.500
in our cells called telomerase.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.060
Telomerase can replenish the lost
bits and elongate our telomeres.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:05.220
So how can we boost this enzyme
to, in effect, reverse cellular aging?

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.460
Exercise may help.
Those with high levels

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.620
of physical activity
have longer telomeres,

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:15.000
whereas obese individuals and smokers
tend to have shorter telomeres,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.520
along with those getting
inadequate sleep.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.800
But what about nutrition?

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.180
Globally, we might expect that any
antioxidant or anti-inflammatory diet

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.100
could be protective for telomeres.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.040
So we're talking like
a whole food plant-based diet

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.940
with a reduced intake of meat and,
in fact, swapping out animal protein

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.640
in general in favor
of plant-based proteins.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.600
Given that plant-based foods
have well-known antioxidant

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.020
and anti-inflammatory effects,
there are fair grounds to believe

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.880
that the consumption
of plant-based foods

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.980
can help to counteract
telomere attrition,

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.440
but you don't know if it actually
would, until you put it to the test.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.220
Dr. Dean Ornish, along
with the Nobel laureate

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.500
who co-discovered
the telomerase enzyme,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.380
studied the effects of
comprehensive lifestyle changes

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.660
on telomerase activity
and telomere length

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.360
using the same plant-based
diet and lifestyle program

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.120
shown to reverse the progression
of heart disease and

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.440
early-stage prostate cancer, and
maybe even early-stage Alzheimer's.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.920
And telomere length shortened
in the control group,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.140
and they aged five years
as expected,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.440
but didn't just not shorten
as much or hold steady,

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.020
but actually lengthened in
the plant-based lifestyle group.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.200
Whereas in a similar study
across a similar time frame,

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.320
there was no difference in
telomere length when just giving

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.740
people the more typical low-fat dairy,
skinless chicken breast,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.320
generic-type
healthier dietary advice.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.700
Antioxidant-rich plant foods
help maintain telomere length.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.060
In contrast, total and saturated fat
intake and consumption

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.460
of refined flour grains,
meat and meat products,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.100
and soda relate
to shorter telomeres.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.620
People eating more
anti-inflammatory diets

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.180
tend to have longer telomeres,

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.660
and the greater the anti-inflammatory
potential of the diet over time,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.300
the greater potential
to significantly slow down

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.640
the rate of telomere shortening.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.340
Those with the most
pro-inflammatory diets

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.760
had almost twice the risk of
accelerated telomere shortening.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.500
The most pro-inflammatory
food component is saturated fat,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.060
found in meat, dairy, eggs and junk,
along with other pro-inflammatory

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.400
food components like
cholesterol and trans fat.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.940
Omega-3's tend to be
anti-inflammatory,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.720
but when put to the test,
fish oil supplements

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.360
failed to have any
significant telomere effects.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.080
The most anti-inflammatory
food component is fiber.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:37.915
And indeed, if you look at dietary
fiber intake and telomere length

00:04:37.915 --> 00:04:40.560
in a representative sampling
of thousands of US adults,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.260
even though nobody
was eating enough,

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.840
the more fiber people consumed,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.820
the longer their telomeres
tended to be.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.180
Since there appeared
to be a straight-line increase,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:52.620
they could do the math,

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:56.760
and it appeared that just a 10-gram
increase in fiber per 1000 calories

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.960
would equate to four fewer years
of biologic aging,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.360
whereas, for example,
the consumption of soda

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.280
appeared to increase cell aging

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.860
by almost two years
per daily serving.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.700
Now, of course, diets high in fiber
and diets with significant amounts

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.340
of fruits, vegetables, and
whole grains go hand in hand.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.740
So fiber may just be a marker

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.720
for eating lots of whole
healthy plant foods,

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.040
but are there any specific plants
associated with telomere lengthening?

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.740
We'll find out next.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.300
Telomere length is considered
a biomarker of aging:

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.420
shorter telomeres are associated
with decreased life expectancy

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:36.420
and increased rates of
age-related chronic diseases.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.360
Telomere shortening has been
shown to be accelerated

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.340
by oxidative stress and inflammation.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.880
So since plant foods contain
plenty of compounds

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.940
with antioxidant and
anti-inflammatory properties,

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.760
it's plausible that their
sustained consumption

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.740
might help counteract
telomere attrition.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:56.820
And indeed, if you pull all the best
studies on the impact of nutrition

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:01.020
on telomere length, the consumption
of vegetables, fruits, beans and nuts

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.140
has been associated with positive
effects on markers of inflammation

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:07.740
and oxidative stress
in parallel with longer telomeres.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:11.760
By contrast, processed meats,
alcoholic and sugar sweetened beverages,

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:14.580
and other foods rich in
saturated fats, alcohol, and sugar

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.740
are linked to an increase
in inflammation

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:20.059
and oxidative stress
in parallel with shorter telomeres.

00:06:20.059 --> 00:06:22.020
In my last video on telomeres,
I featured a randomized controlled trial

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.480
showing a whole food,
plant-based diet and lifestyle program

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:29.880
could actually lengthen telomeres.
Is it just because they cut out the junk?

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.460
Those eating the most ultra-processed
foods have been found

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.860
to have almost twice the odds
of having short telomeres.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.500
Maybe it's because they cut out
the processed meat,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.960
like bacon, ham, hot dogs,
lunchmeat, sausage,

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.260
which has been associated with
not only cancer and diabetes risk

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:47.280
but shorter telomeres as well,
meaning accelerated cellular aging,

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.220
though just having
something like a steak

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:53.100
was not similarly associated
with telomere length.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.700
Processed meat, but not
unprocessed red meat,

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.800
is associated with
shorter telomere length.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.000
This is perhaps due to the particularly
high concentrations of glycotoxins,

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.260
the advanced glycation end products,
as well as carcinogenic nitrosamines

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.340
in the processed meat that may promote
inflammation and oxidative stress.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:17.100
The only unprocessed meat associated
with shorter telomeres was poultry.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.160
For dairy, it appears to be the milk fat.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.680
A national survey of
thousands of Americans

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.680
found an association between
increased biological aging and

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:27.060
the consumption of high-fat milk.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:30.300
Even with people just
going up like 1% milk fat

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.840
(from like 1% milk to 2% milk,
low-fat milk to reduced fat milk)

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:38.160
appeared to have more than 4 years
of additional biological aging.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.840
We think it's because of the saturated
fat, given that saturated fats trigger

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:46.980
an inflammatory response. Not all
plant foods are good for you, though.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.800
French fries and potato chip
consumption is associated

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:51.000
with shorter telomeres.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:55.140
Yes, fiber intake goes hand-in-hand
with longer telomeres,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.360
as does higher vegetable
and fruit consumption,

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.660
but that may be trumped
by a deep fryer.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:05.220
What about the consumption of a
high fat whole plant food like nuts?

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:08.760
We know higher telomere-building
enzyme activity is associated

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.340
with a higher dietary
antioxidant score,

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:14.940
and botanically, seeds are
packed with antioxidants

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:17.940
and by seeds, they mean any food
you can put in the ground

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:22.500
and sprout a whole plant, like whole
grains, beans, chickpeas, lentils, nuts.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:25.680
They're naturally enriched
in antioxidant compounds

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:27.960
that protect the seed
DNA from damage.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.680
Four weeks of Navy beans
didn't seem to affect

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.720
telomere length, though.
What about nuts?

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:37.320
Well, based on how much your
telomeres shorten every year,

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:39.000
you can estimate the rate of aging.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.940
In other words, two people can have
the same chronological age,

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:46.740
same calendar age, but suffer
more or less effective cellular aging.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:49.620
So, for example, if you smoke
a pack a day for a decade,

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.900
your cells may age
about 3 years faster.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:54.180
Or, if you drink soda every day,

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.240
it's like almost 2 years
of additional aging.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.220
So what about nuts?

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:03.240
U.S. adults of the same age would
experience almost 2 years less

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.900
biologic aging per ounce of nuts
and seeds consumed per day,

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:09.900
the amount I recommend
in my free Daily Dozen app.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:14.160
The estimated biologic aging
advantage would be nearly 1 year

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:17.400
for each 100 calories of nuts
and seeds consumed every day.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:21.120
The researchers conclude that, clearly,
consumption of nuts and seeds

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.880
accounts for meaningfully
lower levels of biologic aging

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:27.780
in U.S. men and women.
But that's just an association.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:33.600
You don't know if nuts can slow telomere
shortening until you put it to the test.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.020
A randomized controlled trial
investigating whether the inclusion

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:38.820
of one or two ounces
of walnuts a day

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.940
for two years would help
maintain telomere length,

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:43.440
which normally shortens with age.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.220
In the control group, their
telomeres shortened as expected

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.880
over those two years, whereas the
walnut group telomeres maintained

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:54.720
their length, though the difference
didn't reach statistical significance.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.600
Now, that was measuring
average telomere length,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.700
and it's probably more telling
to look at how long

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:02.520
the shortest telomeres are
rather than the average,

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:05.400
and if you do that, look at
the percentage of telomeres

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:06.420
that are particularly short,

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:09.840
the walnut group does edge
out over the control group.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.020
It's well established that the length
of the shortest telomeres is

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:15.360
the key biomarker
of the onset of senescence.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.360
The researchers conclude
that the inclusion of walnuts

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:20.760
in the regular diet for
2 years tends to delay

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.820
leukocyte telomere shortening
in older individuals.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:27.660
A study on pistachio consumption,
2 ounces a day for 4 months,

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:31.440
reduced signs of DNA damage,
but did not significantly slow

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:33.000
the rate of telomere shortening.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.240
And this study, which randomized
people to eat more mixed nuts,

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:39.840
found a higher risk of telomere
shortening in the nut group,

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:42.720
for which the researchers
could offer no explanation.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:46.860
So it's not clear whether nuts
help with telomeres or not.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:52.080
Most supplement intervention
studies observed null effects

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:55.320
on telomere length as well,
with the exception of green tea.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:59.160
Thirty-six elderly women were
randomly divided into two groups:

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:02.640
exercise alone or exercise with
green tea consumption for 5 months,

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:06.900
and a significant boost in telomere
length in the green tea group

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:08.640
with no change from the placebo.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:14.340
Green tea is essentially a green leafy
vegetable we dip in hot water.

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:18.480
How about eating green leafy vegetables,
in fact the healthiest kind:

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:22.320
cruciferous vegetables like broccoli,
cabbage, collards and kale.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:27.120
They pitted raw versus cooked and
found that cooked was better than raw

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:29.820
for reducing DNA damage
from a carcinogen,

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:35.040
but raw was more anti-inflammatory.
Consequently, to fully exploit

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:37.080
the complexity of the
health-promoting potential

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:40.680
of Ethiopian kale, and by extension
maybe other cruciferous,

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:44.400
a mix of both raw and cooked
vegetables should be part of the diet.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.860
OK, but what about
for boosting

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:49.860
the telomere-lengthening
age-reversal enzyme?

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:53.280
Raw or cooked.
Which do you think?

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:57.900
A short-term dietary intervention
showed that cooked but not raw

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:01.860
boosted telomerase activity
in as short as five days,

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:04.620
eating one and a quarter cups
of this kale a day.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:08.460
It was thought that you'd
need like 4 months of a change

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:10.740
to affect telomeres,
but this study provides,

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:13.440
for the first time, evidence
that telomerase activity

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:17.100
can respond in a matter of days
to a food intervention,

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:21.420
but not just any food,
but the healthiest food out there:

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:24.623
cruciferous dark green
leafy vegetables.

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:25.980
Finally today,

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.920
Dr. Dean Ornish showed
that his plant-based diet,

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.260
exercise, and stress
management intervention

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:34.740
could in effect reverse
the aging of our DNA.

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:39.304
What effect might the stress
management component have had?

00:12:39.481 --> 00:12:45.000
In my Research Into Reversing Aging video,
I highlighted Dean Ornish's landmark study

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.180
showing that a low-fat,&nbsp;
whole foods, plant-based diet&nbsp;

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:50.640
high in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, beans,

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:53.340
along with walking, stress
management, and support

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:57.900
could not only reverse heart, open up
arteries without drugs and surgery,

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:01.260
and potentially reverse the progression
of early-stage prostate cancer,

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:06.960
but was the first intervention EVER
shown to increase telomerase activity,

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:12.660
the enzyme that builds and maintains these caps
at the tips of our chromosomes called telomeres,

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:15.720
which appear to slow the
aging of our cells.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.900
Yes, this new finding was exciting
and should encourage people

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:23.580
to adopt a healthy lifestyle in order to avoid
or combat cancer and age-related diseases,

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:28.320
but was it the diet, the exercise,
or the stress management?

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.140
That's what researchers have
been trying to tease out

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:33.840
in the six years since
this study was published.

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:36.660
Let's look at stress first.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:42.000
In the film "The Holiday",
Cameron Diaz exclaimed,

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:46.260
“Severe stress ... causes the DNA in our cells to
shrink until they can no longer replicate.”

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:49.380
Did Hollywood get
the science right?

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:53.400
Do people who are stressed
have shorter telomeres?

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:57.360
To answer that question, researchers
measured the telomere lengths

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.340
in mothers of
chronically ill children—

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:01.980
what could be more
stressful than that?

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:07.860
The longer a woman had spent being
the main carer of her ill child,

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.560
the shorter were
her telomeres.

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:13.980
The extra telomere shortening
in these stressed mothers

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:17.760
was equivalent to that caused
by at least a decade of aging.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:23.040
We see the same thing in
caregivers of Alzheimer's patients,

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:27.180
and those suffering severe
work related exhaustion.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:32.880
Even those abused as children,
may grow up with shorter telomeres.

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:35.880
Not much we can do
about our past,

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:40.440
but if we manage our stress can we
grow some of our telomeres back?

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:46.500
Well, if you go off to a meditation
retreat and meditate for 500 hours

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:50.100
you can indeed boost your
telomerase activity.

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:54.960
600 hours of meditation may
be beneficial as well,

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.860
but come on, there's got
to be a quicker fix...

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:02.400
and this exciting
new study delivered!

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:05.880
Caregivers of family
members with dementia

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:10.380
randomized to just 12 minutes of
daily meditation for 8 weeks,

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:16.200
so just about 10 hours in total,
experienced significant benefit.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:21.480
Better mental and psychological function
accompanied by an increase in telomerase activity,

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:26.482
suggesting improvement in
stress-induced cellular aging.

